Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs)

APTs are highly skilled at unauthorized network access, often researching targets like large corporations or government entities in advance.
Their motivations include damaging critical infrastructure and gaining access to intellectual property.
Insider Threats and Hacktivists

Insider threats abuse authorized access for sabotage, corruption, espionage, or unauthorized data access.
Hacktivists are driven by political agendas, using digital technology for demonstrations, propaganda, and social change.
Hacker Types

Hackers are individuals who use computers to access systems or data, ranging from beginners to advanced professionals.
They are categorized as authorized (ethical), semi-authorized (researchers), and unauthorized (unethical) based on their adherence to laws and ethical codes.

A hacker is any person who uses computers to gain access to computer systems, networks, or data. They can be beginner or advanced technology professionals who use their skills for a variety of reasons. There are three main categories of hackers:

Authorized hackers are also called ethical hackers. They follow a code of ethics and adhere to the law to conduct organizational risk evaluations. They are motivated to safeguard people and organizations from malicious threat actors.

Semi-authorized hackers are considered researchers. They search for vulnerabilities but don’t take advantage of the vulnerabilities they find.

Unauthorized hackers are also called unethical hackers. They are malicious threat actors who do not follow or respect the law. Their goal is to collect and sell confidential data for financial gain. 
